  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:piZJPtesDeY",
    "purpose": "Test the Ooni Volt electric indoor pizza oven ahead of Kenji's live stage show, timing its preheat and baking a Neapolitan-style pizza from store-bought dough to see how it performs.",
    "video_type": "review",
    "thesis": "Kenji runs the Ooni Volt through a test bake, explaining what distinguishes plug-in pizza ovens limited to about 1,800 watts and showing the full process from preheat through stretching, topping, baking and tasting.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Plug-in home pizza ovens max out around 1,800 watts, so differences between them come down mostly to insulation and heating element arrangement.",
      "The Ooni Volt is heavily insulated, preheats to 850°F, and lets you adjust top and bottom heat independently.",
      "The first pizza in a batch is a 'learning pizza' for finding the oven's hot and cool spots.",
      "Kenji demonstrates pushing out the rim, knuckling and a final stretch on the peel when shaping dough.",
      "He puts basil on before baking, as in traditional Neapolitan pizza, so it perfumes the whole pie.",
      "At 850°F a pizza takes about 90 seconds to 2 minutes; doming the pie boosts top browning.",
      "High heat gives a thin crisp crust with a puffy interior; lower temperatures give a thicker, crisper New York-style crust.",
      "Store-bought Whole Foods dough worked well in the oven."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to Ooni Volt",
        "summary": "Kenji explains why he is testing the Ooni Volt (for the Tasting Notes stage show with Lee Kindell), covers the 1,800-watt limit of plug-in ovens, insulation, and independent top/bottom heat control.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Preheating and dough prep",
        "summary": "He describes the store-bought Whole Foods dough split into 300 g balls and times the preheat to 850°F, then turns the top heat all the way up.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Stretching and topping pizza",
        "summary": "Shaping the dough with semolina, building the rim, knuckling, and a tangent on centripetal vs centrifugal force; then topping with crushed tomatoes, buffalo mozzarella, basil, soppressata, sea salt and olive oil, and a final stretch on the peel.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Cooking the pizza",
        "summary": "Launching the 12-inch pizza, watching the dough puff, and doming it for extra top browning at 850°F.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Tasting and conclusion",
        "summary": "Kenji examines the crumb and spotty bottom, tastes it, explains how heat affects crust texture (Neapolitan vs New York style), recalls his earlier home-oven hacks, and promotes his upcoming shows.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 14
      }
    ],
